// FINDING A — a tor call with NAMED UNION ARMS, placed mid-chain (i.e. NOT
// the first `~`-headed statement of the flow) and INDENTED relative to the
// line that calls it, is misparsed: its own arm lines are reported as
// "stray continuation" rather than recognized as belonging to it. The SAME
// tor called at column 0 (either as the flow's own head, or mid-chain but
// left un-indented) parses correctly. No `unikraft/*` import or Unikraft
// dependency is needed to reproduce this — it is a general parser defect,
// found while writing `unikraft/paging`'s boot test, where every mid-chain
// call to `unikraft/paging:active` (also a zero-arg, two-named-arm tor) hit
// it as soon as the surrounding chain was indented to match the rest of the
// program's nesting.
//
//   koruc --check A_mid_chain_indented_union_call.kz
//   -> error[KORU010]: stray continuation line without Koru construct
//        --> A_mid_chain_indented_union_call.kz:48:9
//            | ready v |> std/io:print.ln("got {{ v:d }}")
//      (and the same for the `unavailable` arm on the next line, :49)
//
// Isolated by bisection: removing the tor's PARAMETER LIST (`{ }` vs one
// scalar param), reordering its arms, giving its EMPTY arm a payload,
// renaming the tor, and swapping which arm carries data all failed to change
// the outcome — the only thing that flips it is the INDENTATION of the call
// and its arms relative to the surrounding chain. See
// `B_mid_chain_indented_union_call_control.kz`: identical program, only the
// indentation of `who`'s call and its two arms is removed (matching the
// column of the `|>` that reaches it), and it compiles clean.
//
// Practical consequence for a Koru author: a mid-chain call to a tor with
// its own named arms must be written at the SAME indentation as the
// enclosing flow's other top-level arms, not nested deeper to visually
// track the chain — `unikraft/paging/tests/boot_paging.kz` works around this
// by never calling `unikraft/paging:active` mid-chain at all, always as a
// fresh top-level flow statement (matching `unikraft/vmem`'s own two-flow
// convention, which happens to never have exercised this shape).

~import std/io

~pub tor who { }
| ready i32
| unavailable

~proc who|zig {
    return .{ .ready = 1 };
}

~std/io:print.ln("before")
    |> who
        | ready v |> std/io:print.ln("got {{ v:d }}")
        | unavailable |> std/io:print.ln("none")
